/**
 * @page topic_av Audio/Video Networking
 * @ingroup module_network
 * @brief The current media path from capture and mixing to ACIP delivery.
 *
 * Video and audio share the ACIP transport but use different admission and
 * processing paths. Control packets are reliable and ordered; disposable media
 * is bounded and may be dropped under pressure.
 *
 * @section av_video Video
 *
 * Clients capture or decode frames, encode when the selected format requires it,
 * and send image or encoded-frame messages through the client ACIP API. Servers
 * collect participant frames, create per-client layouts, render to terminal
 * capabilities, and send ASCII or encoded output. The renderer consumes decoded
 * frame buffers; it does not know about sockets.
 *
 * @section av_audio Audio
 *
 * Audio capture produces PCM or Opus batches. The server keeps per-client input
 * buffers, mixes other participants, and sends PCM/Opus batches to each peer.
 * The client playback callback reads a bounded jitter buffer and outputs silence
 * on underrun. The old single-sample packet remains only for compatibility.
 *
 * @section av_backpressure Admission
 *
 * Media producers send through the shared outbound scheduler. It prioritizes
 * control, then audio, then visual traffic and applies transport-specific
 * datagram preferences without creating parallel media queues in each mode.
 * Failed admission is an explicit error; producers must stop or drop according
 * to the media policy rather than blocking a realtime loop.
 *
 * @see topic_network
 * @see topic_packet_types
 * @see topic_client_capture
 * @see topic_client_audio
 */
